/-
# Composition factors of 2-closures of solvable groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 19.67)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.67 (I. Ponomarenko): let `G ≤ Sym(Ω)` with
`Ω` finite. The `2`-closure `G⁽²⁾` is the largest subgroup of `Sym(Ω)` containing `G` with the same
orbits as `G` on `Ω × Ω`. Is it true that if `G` is solvable, then every composition factor of
`G⁽²⁾` is cyclic or alternating?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.SpecificGroups.Alternating
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.KourovkaNotebookShared

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.TwoClosureSolvableCompositionFactorsKourovka19Problem67Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.67 (I.
Ponomarenko). UnsolvedMath record UM-00701615 (`KOU-19.67`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

`G⁽²⁾` is the set of permutations mapping every pair `(α, β)` into its `G`-orbit (written as the
subgroup it generates, which it already is). A composition factor of `G⁽²⁾` is a simple group onto
which a subnormal subgroup maps; "alternating" means isomorphic to some `alternatingGroup (Fin n)`.
`IsCompositionFactor` is the shared definition in
`WantedExt/GroupTheory/KourovkaNotebookShared.lean`.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.KourovkaNotebookShared

/-- **The $2$-closure of a permutation group.** For a subgroup $G$ of the symmetric group
$\mathrm{Sym}(\Omega)$, the $2$-closure $G^{(2)}$ of $G$ is the subgroup of $\mathrm{Sym}(\Omega)$
generated by the permutations $\sigma$ that map every pair of points into its $G$-orbit, that is,
such that for all $\alpha, \beta \in \Omega$ there is $g \in G$ with $g(\alpha) = \sigma(\alpha)$
and $g(\beta) = \sigma(\beta)$.

These permutations already form a subgroup: the largest subgroup of $\mathrm{Sym}(\Omega)$
containing $G$ with the same orbits as $G$ on $\Omega \times \Omega$. -/
def twoClosure {Ω : Type*} (G : Subgroup (Equiv.Perm Ω)) : Subgroup (Equiv.Perm Ω) :=
  Subgroup.closure {σ | ∀ a b : Ω, ∃ g ∈ G, g a = σ a ∧ g b = σ b}

/-- **Kourovka Problem 19.67.** The statement that for every finite set $\Omega$ and every
solvable subgroup $G$ of the symmetric group $\mathrm{Sym}(\Omega)$, every composition factor of
the $2$-closure $G^{(2)}$ of $G$ is cyclic or isomorphic to the alternating group $A_n$ for some
natural number $n$. -/
def solvableTwoClosureFactorsCyclicOrAlternating : Prop :=
  ∀ (Ω : Type) [Finite Ω] (G : Subgroup (Equiv.Perm Ω)), Group.IsSolvable G →
    ∀ (S : Type) [Group S], IsCompositionFactor S (twoClosure G) →
      IsCyclic S ∨ ∃ n : ℕ, Nonempty (S ≃* alternatingGroup (Fin n))

/--
**Skresanov's counterexample to Kourovka Problem 19.67.** There exist a finite set $\Omega$, a
solvable subgroup $G$ of the symmetric group $\mathrm{Sym}(\Omega)$, and a composition factor $S$
of the $2$-closure $G^{(2)}$ of $G$ such that $S$ is not cyclic and $S$ is not isomorphic to the
alternating group $A_n$ for any $n$. Here $G^{(2)}$ is the subgroup of $\mathrm{Sym}(\Omega)$
generated by the permutations $\sigma$ such that for all $\alpha, \beta \in \Omega$ some $g \in G$
has $g(\alpha) = \sigma(\alpha)$ and $g(\beta) = \sigma(\beta)$, and a composition factor of
$G^{(2)}$ is a simple group onto which some subnormal subgroup of $G^{(2)}$ maps.

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.67 (I.
Ponomarenko); resolution: S. V. Skresanov, Counterexamples to two conjectures in the Kourovka
notebook, Algebra Logic 58 (2019), no. 3, 249-253, abstract: counterexamples to Questions 12.78 and
19.67 of the Kourovka Notebook; Kourovka Archive, Problem 19.67: No, it is not true,
https://doi.org/10.1007/s10469-019-09543-1.
-/
public theorem_wanted solvableTwoClosureFactorsCyclicOrAlternating_refuted :
    ¬ solvableTwoClosureFactorsCyclicOrAlternating

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.TwoClosureSolvableCompositionFactorsKourovka19Problem67Wanted
